hey guys Greg knuckles here back with  the 11th I think installment of the  string theory semi daily Q&amp;A series and  today's question is in my opinion what  are some of the biggest gaps facing  exercise in sports science right now in  the scientific literature so there are  just right off the top of my head two  Biggie's that come to mind the first  would be that we don't have all that  much really really long-term data and  the second is that we have a lot of data  about you know factors that are going to  be better for most people most of the  time on average but not all that much  work has been done about how to optimize  a training program for the individual so  the first issue the fact that we don't  have just a ton of long-term data this  isn't something that I hold against the  scientific community at all because work  like that it's it's incredibly expensive  and unwieldy to do so there are a lot of  studies looking to see you know what  produces the best results over six weeks  eight weeks 12 weeks 16 weeks sum all  the way out to 20 weeks and you know  that's a pretty decent chunk of time  like that's going to be a training cycle  or longer for for most people you know  there may be some Olympic athletes  watching this on a quadrennial plan but  for most of us you know we're only  planning training 12 16 weeks in advance  most of the time so that is you know  that that is very good helpful data on  that time scale but something to keep in  mind is that most of the studies are  maxing out at 12 to 16 weeks or so so  that makes it difficult to say with just  a ton of confidence that one that one  approach is going to produce a lot  better results than another approach  over you know two three five ten years  it very well could be that something  produces really quick results in the  short term and then the results kind of  peter out whereas something  produces kind of more moderate results  over a longer period of time and so  there very well may not be meaningful  long term differences between those two  different approaches that's just  something that's that we can't really  say with a ton of confidence right now  just because there aren't many you know  two three four year training studies  being done that would be one incredibly  hard to recruit people for say like hey  turn your training over to me for the  next half a decade most people are going  to do that - it's gonna be a really  expensive study to run and then three  it's just gonna be a huge pain in the  ass so there's not a ton of stuff out  there like that right now which does  which does limit the timescales that we  can speak confidently on science is  really really good at sports science  exercise science at this point telling  us what's probably going to work well  you know over the next three or four  months but then past that straight up we  are just kind of extrapolating from  there and then the second issue that  there's a ton of data about what  produces superior results on average for  most people most of the time but not all  that much about how to individualize a  training plan so the way science  typically works in this field is you'll  have one group on one exercise plan one  group on another exercise plan and  typically from a control group as well  doing nothing and then you know after  three or four months of training you see  which one of those protocols produced  better results on average so you're  taking mean standard deviations most  research still works with p-value so a  frequentist statistical approach to see  at a pea level of less than 0.05 which  one of these produce better results so  that's that's how most research is done  but that doesn't necessarily mean that  the protocol that one out is going to be  better for everyone it just produces on  average the best results there is some  work being done there was a study by  even and a more recent one blanking on  the researchers name maybe Brown  I'll link them in the description but  yeah there is some research being done  showing well I guess kind of validating  that you know simply put some things  work better for for some people than  other people what may be kind of the  optimal thing based on our current  scientific understanding that's probably  going to produce good results for the  majority of people but it may be very  inappropriate for some people whereas  something that may not be as  scientifically validated may very well  produce better results for a minority of  people and there's there's very very  little work in that area being done thus  far and again that's that's primarily a  logistical problem as well because to do  that type of research you would need  crossover studies so you would need two  groups group one does protocol a group  two does protocol be four twelve sixteen  weeks whatever then have a pretty  lengthy washout period in between and  then group one does protocol B through  to does protocol a and then kind of  tease out what factors separated the  people who did best on protocol a versus  who did best on protocol be with  everyone doing both of the protocols at  some point during the study again those  studies they take twice as long to do  there gonna be more than twice as  expensive much harder to recruit people  for so again I I'm certainly not  begrudging the science at the community  for not having more of those types of  studies because yeah there would just be  a huge pain in the ass but to this point  there's an just not a ton of that type  of data to to really look at  individualizing approaches based on the  scientific literature and so really to  this point that's that's not an  incredibly huge drawback because with  what I've always recommended is using  the scientific data as a starting point  and then just personalizing your own  training program your clients training  programs from there  based on how they're responding to it so  at this point you know training yourself  or training other people it is just as  much of an art as a science so you know  you have that starting point you can get  that from science but then personalizing  it that's going to have to come from you  and your own experiences and you know  how you're responding to your training  how your clients are responding to their  training so yeah as I see it those are  right now the two biggest gaps in the  scientific literature for people trying  to use a very very purely scientific  approach to planning their own training  they're planning their clients training  and again neither neither one of those  things are things that I hold against  the scientific community at all because  getting that type of data would be very  expensive and very laborious but as it  stands now those are I think the two the  two biggest issues so you know for for  at least a while there's going to be  plenty plenty of space for lifters  coaches you know to apply their own  experiences on top of the scientific  literature to you know really optimize  training for yourself for your clients  so that is the video if you liked it  please like and subscribe and if you  have a question you want answered ask it  in the comments below and if it is my  favorite I will answer it in that next  video alright until next time guys hope  you have a good day hope your training  is good and I'll see you in the next  video
 

